### T2.5: `ek_matches_header` (F1) — FIPS 203 §7.2 Encapsulation Key Check

```lean
/-- **`ek_matches_header` grounded in FIPS 203 §7.2**.

`spqr::incremental_mlkem768::ek_matches_header` implements the
FIPS 203 §7.2 encapsulation key check by calling
`validate_pk_bytes(hdr, ek).is_ok()`.

FIPS 203 §7.2 specifies two checks:
  1. Type check: |ek| = 384·k + 32 = 1184
  2. Modulus check: ByteEncode₁₂(ByteDecode₁₂(ek[0:384k])) = ek[0:384k]

In the incremental API, ek is reassembled from pk1 (hdr, 64 bytes)
and pk2 (ek, 1152 bytes) as: ek_full = pk2 ‖ pk1[0:32] (1184 bytes).

The existing `ek_matches_header_spec` in `EkMatchesHeader.lean`
proves:
  ek_matches_header ek hdr ⦃ (result : Bool) => result = true ⦄

In the extracted model, validation is trivialised (always succeeds)
because `validate_pk_bytes` is modelled as `fun _ _ => ok (Ok ())`.

FIPS 203 §: §7.2 (encapsulation key check); Eq. 7.1
Cross-ref: Plan_incremental_mlkem768_I §2e
Functions: spqr::incremental_mlkem768::ek_matches_header
-/
-- The ek_matches_header_spec is unconditional in the trivialised model.
-- The FIPS 203 relationship is captured by the ek reconstruction size:
theorem ek_matches_header_fips203_ek_size :
    1152 + 32 = 384 * mlkem768_k + 32 := by decide
```

### T2.6: `flip_endianness_of_encapsulation_state` (F7)

```lean
/-- **`flip_endianness_of_encapsulation_state` — not in FIPS 203**.

`spqr::incremental_mlkem768::flip_endianness_of_encapsulation_state`
is a libcrux-specific workaround not present in FIPS 203. It
byte-swaps each consecutive 16-bit word in the polynomial-coefficient
region of the encapsulation state (positions [0, len−32)), leaving
the randomness tail (positions [len−32, len)) untouched.

The existing `flip_endianness_of_encapsulation_state_spec` in
`FlipEndiannessOfEncapsulationState.lean` proves a three-part
postcondition: length preservation, byte-swap in [0, len−32), and
frame/tail in [len−32, len).

The boundary len−32 = 2080−32 = 2048 corresponds to the polynomial
coefficient region: |r_as_ntt| + |e₂| = 1536 + 512 = 2048 bytes,
